## Scope And Reuse

Original 21 means original source question 21, not an assumed count of 21 new
choice questions. Additions are derived from the combined draft and must all
trace only to original 21. All ten retained choice views must be unchanged.

The retained HTML/draft order maps displayed question 2 to `n3-13`, original 13.
Both old observations explicitly cover that question's stem/material a-i/options
A-I. Its exact retained section hash, semantic view hash, original ordinal and
current CSS are bound. Its material is removed before the renderer call; neither
it nor an old screenshot is reopened, rerendered or recaptured.

Original 12 is only partially observed: desktop A-F and mobile F are inherited.
Its heading/stem and mobile A-E remain missing. Those visible option regions are
excluded from new per-view screenshot material only after full section-byte and
CSS binding. None of this calls original 12 an inherited whole-question PASS.

Known preparation HOLD: the retained `.nb,.sr-nb` rule uses `padding-block:.2em`,
but the current renderer literal uses `padding-block:calc(.2em + 1px)`. A current
CSS equality policy stops before the renderer. Only a parent who explicitly
chooses `preserve-old-pass-current-css-hold-no-recapture` in the approved receipt
can allow independent gap capture with this mismatch. That policy retains the
old observed regions solely under their exact old CSS, does not promote them to
current-CSS PASS, and never adds a capture of n3-13 or original-12 desktop A-F /
mobile F. The current inherited-CSS HOLD remains visible in the report.

There are nine remaining old questions plus the parent-approved original-21
addition(s). The four complete n3 text/written PASS combinations are referenced,
not rendered or captured. No answer files, source-solving, chats, DB, environment
files, network, Git, dependency installation, shared tool or durable output edits.

## Rendering And Observation Boundary

The three approved combined files are first copied byte-for-byte to an immutable
scratch snapshot. Renderer input seed/review and current lesson/exercises/figures
are same-byte copies. A separate explicitly recorded disposable gap draft removes
only n3-13 and original-12 F before the one `--choices-only` call; this is a display projection, not
a producer revision, re-review, or re-bound review approval. Remaining displayed
ordinal headings are restored to the approved combined ordering in scratch HTML.
Original-12 F is reconstructed from its retained HTML bytes for the whole-section
byte-identity check only, never by rendering. Both navigated pages exclude it;
the desktop page also excludes A-E, which was rendered once solely for mobile.

Original fig-01 SVG/spec/render/review are copied byte-for-byte to the scratch
edition figure directory. Existing figure approval is reused by hash only. Browser
checks actual SVG screen transformations for every label >=16 px and native
full-media glyph/tile reachability and media union. No figure producer or scientific
figure review is called.

One installed app Playwright Chromium launch, one desktop context and one mobile
context, one file navigation each. No HTTP server or external request is needed.
Native viewport screenshots, glyph/math-rule coverage, table text/formula
projections and media-region unions can fail. No fullpage, crop or montage.
Each view writes a target inventory before its assertions. A small caller adapter
records every produced screenshot's path/hash and actual count before the exact
inherited capture helper's decoding or coverage assertions can fail.

Every new screenshot stays UNOBSERVED. The parent must dispatch fresh independent
batches of at most eight images. Capture geometry success is not visual PASS.
The first fault and any produced bytes are preserved; no automatic retries,
renderer reruns, producer reruns or validator calls. An existing runtime-FIRST
directory prevents a second invocation. Renderer and browser contexts are closed
in finally; failures in cleanup are reported as FIRST failures.
